{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AKZSMiV6UBkXLzFzh8LdwA","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (48.7KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M86QTPQF74VVF77RZ2E23RAE/7502165d-e97d-4c69-8e49-b7b74508d8a9/tool-results/bnbsy2oi3.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n39:internal/prgate/            reviews-passed-at-head decision shared by the reviewer's mark-ready and the merger: label rule, head Covers, reviewed heads, ParseWorkerBranch\n41:internal/merger/            the merger loop: gates (reviews, checks from check runs and the rules API, protected-path approval), one update and one squash merge per tick; no slot\n5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result, or a scanner at filing (`route: needs-human`) | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38b45f0e-1977-46fe-92b9-68df068f9fb5","uuid":"86cf3ccc-fce9-45f1-af65-6a05fe9f2cab","timestamp":"2026-11-24T12:25:37.270Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:internal/prgate/            reviews-passed-at-head decision shared by the reviewer's mark-ready and the merger: label rule, head Covers, reviewed heads, ParseWorkerBranch\n41:internal/merger/            the merger loop: gates (reviews, checks from check runs and the rules API, protected-path approval), one update and one squash merge per tick; no slot\n5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result, or a scanner at filing (`route: needs-human`) | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n129:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n138:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n152:## Open issues\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n166:## Scanners\n183:**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pending` (default for executable scanners) files with `ard:pending`, so they enter triage; `none` (default for LLM scanners) files with the scanner label only, and a human adds `ard:pending` to send one issue to triage; `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` and no `ard:pending`, so no agent triages them. LLM scanners may not use `pending`. Changing `route` affects only new issues. A human promotes one issue by removing `ard:needs-human` and/or adding `ard:pending`; after that ard never joins findings onto it.\n188:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n206:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n229:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; only HEAD is scanned, and a history scanner is a follow-up (#224). The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n235:## Triage loop\n241:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n251:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\n253:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 2,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n255:**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\n257:Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\n259:Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n265:| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\n272:Each triage role owns one `##` section of `plan.md` with a fixed heading (Requirements, Design, UX, Docs, Implementation; a custom triage role names its own with `plan_section`). The first writer creates the file with `# Plan for #<issue#>`. A role replaces its own section in place on a re-run, never edits another role's section (close-out enforces it), and leaves the file untouched on `blocked` or `needs-human`.\n276:## Executors and the Reaper\n284:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n299:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\n301:**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).\n316:## Worker loop\n320:1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\n321:2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\n324:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. The changed lines of the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) are checked against two per-worker-role thresholds. Over `diff_max_lines` (default 2,000) also holds, as `diff guard: N changed lines, limit M`. Over `diff_warn_lines` (default 1,000) but not over the limit, the PR goes ahead: the loop logs `worker.diff_large` (lines, warn, max), and the PR body and the close-out issue comment carry a fixed note, `Large change: N changed lines (warn at W)`, so reviewers know; the note quotes nothing from the change. The latest step's count decides, so a fix round that shrinks the PR drops the note. The comment shows a rejection reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n327:8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<ro","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M86QTPQF74VVF77RZ2E23RAE/7502165d-e97d-4c69-8e49-b7b74508d8a9/tool-results/bnbsy2oi3.txt","persistedOutputSize":49878},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AKZSMiV6UBkXLzFzh8LdwA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
